# pattern_fnoise.ftsl — filtered (band-limited) fBm (O8).
#
#   fnoise(x, y, z, w, octaves)
#
# The same lattice `noise`, summed over `octaves` at lacunarity 2 and gain 0.5, with
# each octave weighted by how much of it a shading sample OF WIDTH w can actually
# resolve. `w` is in the units of the coordinates you pass in, and is the diameter of
# the surface patch that one sample stands for. `w <= 0` is plain, unfiltered fBm.
#
# WHAT THIS SCENE IS FOR. Every other noise in ftrace is evaluated at a point. That is
# a lie the moment one shading sample stands for an area: once a feature is smaller than
# the footprint, point-sampling reports one arbitrary member of the population instead
# of the population's mean. The detail does not politely fade out — it FOLDS DOWN into a
# coarse pattern of the sampling lattice, and no amount of extra samples removes it,
# because you are sampling a signal that was never band-limited in the first place.
#
# So the floor here runs away to the horizon, split down the middle of the frame. (The
# camera looks down +z with +y up, so world +x is screen-LEFT — mind that when reading
# the expression below.)
#
#     LEFT  of frame (x > 0)   plain fBm, point-sampled. Crisp underfoot; past a few
#                     metres it stops resolving and just keeps a fixed hash of
#                     pixel-scale speckle all the way to the horizon, with nothing to
#                     do with the texture. In a flyby that speckle crawls.
#     RIGHT of frame (x < 0)   the identical field through `fnoise`, with the footprint
#                     handed in. At the bottom edge of the frame it keeps 93% of the
#                     coarsest octave and roughly half of the finest — the near field
#                     reads as the same material — and from there it settles smoothly
#                     toward the mean instead of breaking up.
#
# Measured off the render — mean |pixel - its 3x3 mean| over each half, by row, which
# is a direct read of how much pixel-to-pixel hash a row carries:
#
#     distance    3.8 m   5.4 m   7.8 m    15 m    26 m   horizon
#     plain        11.2     9.9     9.4    11.0     9.7      9.4
#     filtered      9.3     6.4     3.6     0.7     0.3      0.0
#     ratio        1.21x   1.56x   2.64x     16x     28x    >100x
#
# Read the `plain` row, not the ratio: it does not fall with distance AT ALL. A texture
# 26 m away, whose features are a hundredth of a pixel across, still delivers exactly as
# much pixel-scale contrast as the one underfoot. That flat line IS the aliasing — the
# folded-down energy belongs to the sampling lattice, not to the texture, so minifying
# further cannot shrink it and neither can a bigger render. The `filtered` row is what
# minification is supposed to look like.
#
# Render it in mode W (deterministic, one sample per pixel), which is where this
# matters:
#
#   ftrace -in scenes/pattern_fnoise.ftsl -mode W -spp 1 -r 900 600 \
#          -window -keepwindow -o png/pattern_fnoise.png
#
# The forward photon modes do NOT need this. There each pixel accumulates millions of
# photons whose hit points are spread all over its footprint, so the texture is already
# area-averaged — stochastically, for free — and `fnoise` would only cost detail. It is
# the deterministic samplers that need it: mode W, the raster preview, and low-spp
# backward renders.

scene {
    units    meters
    spectral 360 830 1
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FOOTPRINT. `fnoise` needs to be told how wide the sample is, and until the
# renderer hands that over as a pattern variable it has to be written out. It is worth
# writing out once anyway, because it shows what the quantity actually is.
#
# The camera stands at eye height h = 1.6 looking down the floor, so for a floor point
# at distance d from it:
#
#   * one pixel subtends a fixed angle a = fov_y / res_y, so the footprint ACROSS the
#     view direction is a*d — it grows linearly with distance;
#   * the floor is seen at a grazing angle, sin(theta) = h/d, which stretches the
#     footprint ALONG the view direction by d/h, giving a*d^2/h.
#
# The footprint is therefore a long thin ellipse, not a disc, and a single scalar width
# has to choose. This uses the geometric mean sqrt(a*d * a*d^2/h) = a*d^1.5/sqrt(h),
# the standard compromise: the minor axis alone would leave the long direction aliased,
# the major axis alone would smear the short one to mush. (Filtering too hard is the
# worse mistake of the two — see REFERENCE.md — so when in doubt lean to the minor.)
#
# With fov_y 40 deg = 0.6981 rad over 600 rows, a = 1.16355e-3, and 1/sqrt(1.6) =
# 1.26491, so the coefficient is 9.1987e-4. d^2 = x^2 + z^2 + h^2 (the camera is at the
# origin in x and z, so this is exact rather than an approximation of `r`).
#
#   NOTE the factor of 90 on the width. The noise is sampled at 90*x, so a footprint of
#   w metres is 90*w in the space the noise lives in. Getting this wrong is the easiest
#   mistake to make with `fnoise`: the width must be scaled exactly as the coordinates
#   are, or the filter is off by that factor and either does nothing or erases the
#   texture.
#
# WHY 3 OCTAVES AT BASE 90 AND NOT A CLASSIC WIDE fBm. This took a re-render to get
# right and is worth knowing before authoring with `fnoise`. In a gain-0.5 octave stack
# the fine octaves carry almost no amplitude — the seventh is 1/64 of the first — and
# the fine octaves are exactly the ones a distant sample cannot resolve. So a wide fBm
# both ALIASES faintly and FILTERS faintly: at 5 m the first attempt here (7 octaves at
# base 9) still resolved its dominant octave perfectly, the filter correctly left it
# alone, and the two halves of the frame were indistinguishable. That is the primitive
# being right, not the demo being right.
#
# What aliases visibly is a texture whose energy sits AT the resolution limit — which is
# also the common case in practice: grain, weave, gravel, stucco, sandpaper. Base 90/m
# puts the coarsest octave at 1.1 cm — half-period 5.6 mm, which the footprint outgrows
# at d = 3.3 m, about 2.9 m out along the floor and just past the bottom edge of the
# frame. So every octave is undersampled over nearly the whole visible floor and the
# transition lands where it can be seen.

# `step(0, x)` is 1 where x > 0, i.e. over the LEFT half of the frame (see the note on
# handedness above), so the left half gets the raw field and the right half the filtered
# one. Everything else about the two sides — the lattice, the octave count, the
# material, the light — is identical, so every difference in the image is the filtering
# and nothing else. The two `fnoise` calls differ ONLY in the fourth operand.
pattern "floor_split" {
    expr "0.18 + 0.62 * mix(fnoise(90*x, 90*y + 0.5, 90*z,
                                   90 * 9.1987e-4 * pow(x*x + z*z + 2.56, 0.75), 3),
                            fnoise(90*x, 90*y + 0.5, 90*z, 0, 3),
                            step(0, x))"
}

material "floor" { type diffuse  reflect pattern:floor_split }
material "grey"  { type diffuse  reflect rgb 0.35 0.35 0.35 }

# A floor 300 m wide and 400 m long — wide enough that it still fills the frame at the
# far end, and long enough that the far end is far beyond the resolving distance.
quad { origin -150 0 -5   u 0 0 405   v 300 0 0   material floor }

# A low marker wall at the far end, so the eye has something to read the horizon
# against and the floor does not simply run off into the sky.
quad { origin -150 0 400   u 300 0 0   v 0 6 0   material grey }

light sun {
    elevation 38   azimuth 15
    angle     0.53
    spd       preset:bb5800
    intensity 3
}

camera "cam" {
    eye 0 1.6 0   look_at 0 0 12   up 0 1 0   fov_y 40
    mode W
    film { res 900 600 }
}
